# .draugr.conf: `dr-forge gateway` GENERATED it. After you edit this file, another run
# reports it as "differs, kept". -y overwrites it.
#
# The gateway runs no agent. It holds one allow-all proxy, and the sbx policy filters
# its egress. Before you add something that filters, read README.md.
DRAUGR_AGENT=shell
DRAUGR_SANDBOX=@SANDBOX@
DRAUGR_MEMORY=2g                          # default is 50% of host RAM, capped at 32 GiB
DRAUGR_CPUS=2
# 18888 is the proxy. 18445 is SMB, reached the same way, for the same reason. The
# host tunnel carries it into the guest, and the guest mounts the vault through it.
# The guest connects to 445, the only port that its SMB client uses. Thus the tunnel
# maps guest 445 to this one.
#
# A PUBLISHED 18888 MAKES THIS MOUND THE GATEWAY. dr-forge reads this key to decide
# whether this repository is the gateway. It must answer that while all is down. Thus
# it is a declared fact here, not a probe.
#
# AND 18445 IS HERE ONLY IF THIS GATEWAY SERVES A VAULT. On the host, the publisher
# binds a published port, not the service behind it. On a gateway with no share, the
# host then answers for a service that does not run. The vault phase of `dr-layer
# provision` reads that listener. It decides from it whether to install a share
# credential and REBOOT the guest. A port that lies costs a reboot. docs/FORGE.md,
# "2b's own five decisions".
DRAUGR_PORTS="@PORTS@"
DRAUGR_KIT=".draugr/kit kits/unreal kits/base"   # repo-relative, all kept here
DRAUGR_MEM_SYNC=off
# Git tracks nothing here, so there is nothing to commit, review, or push. The
# gateway comes from the template of Draugr, and is generated again when the work
# changes. The first Draugr command that reaches this directory creates the
# repository under this key. docs/FORGE.md, "The gateway, generated".
DRAUGR_ON_MISSING_REPO=create-data-only
# The machine-wide configuration can mount things into each mound. The gateway runs
# no agent, so it gets only what it needs: the two directories that it serves to the
# VM. They are the vault holder, read-only, and one scratch directory that the VM can
# write. A mound reads each byte of its mounts, so these are exact names, never a
# whole drive.
DRAUGR_MOUNTS="@MOUNTS@"
DRAUGR_ENV=
# NOTHING TRAVELS. A gateway holds a proxy and an allowlist, and the mound already mounts
# this directory read-only. Thus no data goes in or out, and dr-up must not try. Empty on
# purpose, not absent. A reader must not need to know the default of an absent key. In
# data-only mode, an absent key once meant "everything".
DRAUGR_DATA=
# The VM of this gateway, so that dr-forge also works from here.
DRAUGR_FORGE=@VM@
# NEVER STOPPED WHEN IDLE, because nothing can measure "idle" here. docs/FORGE.md, "Two
# mounds, two policies": *the gateway must be up whenever the VM is*. Its user is a Windows
# VM on the host. That is not a mound, so it cannot hold a claim. dr-dep counts a claim
# only while the mound of the CLAIMANT runs. Thus a forge controlled from the host has no
# valid claimant. Then the 10m default sees an engine install of some hours as "nobody
# wants the gateway".
#
# A TEST SHOWED THIS, and it cost an afternoon. An engine download lost its proxy after 16
# minutes, to `dr-dep: nobody has used <gateway> for 10m; stopping it`. The ssh tunnel of
# the guest continued to forward 3128 to a host port that did not listen. The only symptom
# was a connection problem in the Launcher.
#
# To stop a gateway, use `dr-stop`. That is correct, because it stays up while agent
# mounds come and go. If you want it to expire, set a duration here.
DRAUGR_LINGER=off
